// The emission control: a user enum sharing no name with anything in the
// prelude, used by nothing.
//
// Whatever decides which declarations survive has to leave this file alone. The
// rule being pinned here widens what reaches the output - a type now earns its
// declaration by being NAMED rather than by being built - and a rule stated one
// notch too wide would keep this enum too. The reference drops it, so the
// emitted C is the assertion; both compilers accept the file and print the same
// thing whether or not the declaration is there.

enum Zed { A | B }

fn main() { println(1); }
